From: Runeon Contributors <dev@runeon.com>
Subject: [PATCH] kernelbase: Support code page 54936 (GB18030).

Windows has supported code page 54936 since Windows XP, through code
rather than an NLS table, because the table format has no four-byte
sequences. Wine has no table for it, so IsValidCodePage(54936) fails and
MultiByteToWideChar() and WideCharToMultiByte() return 0 with
ERROR_INVALID_PARAMETER (WineHQ bug 45339). Programs that convert their
own strings as GB18030 then get empty strings; one such program passes an
empty class name to CreateWindowExW() and never shows a window.

Implement the conversion as GB18030-2000, the mapping Windows has used:
single bytes are ASCII; two-byte codes reuse the code page 936 table
except for 81 codes that GB18030 maps differently, such as 0xA2E3 to the
euro sign and the 0xFE5x radicals that GBK leaves in the private use area;
four-byte codes map the rest of the BMP through 206 ranges of consecutive
code points and the supplementary planes linearly from 0x90308130.

Flags are validated as for UTF-8, by the existing checks: Windows
documents only MB_ERR_INVALID_CHARS and WC_ERR_INVALID_CHARS for this code
page, but rejecting the default MB_PRECOMPOSED that callers commonly pass
would bring back the empty strings, so other accepted flags are ignored.
Invalid input decodes to U+FFFD and a lone surrogate encodes to the
default character, unless MB_ERR_INVALID_CHARS or WC_ERR_INVALID_CHARS
asks for an error. GetCPInfo() reports a maximum character size of 4 and
no lead bytes. These error and GetCPInfo() details follow the UTF-8 path
and were not compared with Windows.

The change is not opt-in: it only makes calls that always failed succeed,
and every other code page takes the same path as before.

